# Our answer

Full write-up: [`FULFILLMENT_SPONSORS.md` → VoloRidge](../FULFILLMENT_SPONSORS.md#voloridge--public-data-challenge-a-simple-signal-and-the-nulls-that-test-it)
Code, figures, data: [`eeg-state-detection/`](../eeg-state-detection/README.md)

**The final model is 84 numbers and a logistic regression. Everything we added on top of it
lost, except one parameter. We know the signal is real because we built two null
distributions that could have said otherwise, and on the second problem they did.**

## The dataset

[Shin et al. 2018, dataset A](https://doc.ml.tu-berlin.de/simultaneous_EEG_NIRS/)
([Scientific Data](https://doi.org/10.1038/sdata.2018.3)). 28 EEG channels plus 2 eye
channels at 1000 Hz, n-back working memory. Participants VP002 to VP006, three sessions
each, unmodified, experimenters' own markers as ground truth. Not committed;
`scripts/fetch_shin.py` downloads and CRC-verifies it.

## Extracting the signal

```text
28 channels, 200 Hz
  → regress out the two eye channels          (fitted on a pre-task segment, then frozen)
  → log power in theta / alpha / beta          (2 s windows, every 0.25 s → 84 numbers)
  → logistic regression, task vs rest          (regularization chosen on whole blocks)
  → exponential moving average of the margin   (one parameter: the half-life)
  → margin ≥ 0 for 3 s becomes an interval
```

## Elegance: everything more complex lost

Seven alternatives, each a paired comparison on the same recordings, same windows, same
folds. Rows are different participants, so this is deliberately not a leaderboard; each row
is only valid against its own baseline.

| Added on top of band power | Complexity | Paired result | Kept |
|---|---|---|:--:|
| Riemannian covariance + tangent space | 1218 features vs 84 | 22.2 s of rest flagged against 2.0 s | ✗ |
| EWMA volatility features | 3× features | Better ranking, worse balanced accuracy | ✗ |
| Broad-band Fourier power | different estimator | A tie, for more machinery | ✗ |
| 26 one-Hz Fourier bins | 728 features vs 84 | 0.541 vs 0.635 balanced accuracy | ✗ |
| Bayesian DLM, AR(1) noise | latent state | 0.739 vs 0.791 for a plain moving average | ✗ |
| Adaptive IMM switching DLM | two regimes | 0.781 vs 0.791 for a plain moving average | ✗ |
| Zigzag persistent homology | 108 descriptors | p = 0.13 against randomly placed flags | ✗ |
| **Exponential moving average** | **one parameter** | **7 of 9 whole tasks vs 3 of 9, same weights** | **✓** |

## Validation

**Circular time-shift null.** The intact score trace shifted against the labels 2000 times
per session. Shifting preserves autocorrelation; shuffling destroys it, and overlapping EEG
windows are not independent samples. The null intervals reach 0.8 AUROC and we report that
rather than using a test that would have flattered us.

**Random-flag null.** Flags thrown at random on the same grid, same budget, same separation,
same matching tolerance. Chance is not zero: five random flags match about 3 of 36 events.

**Eye-channel control**, the same pipeline on channels with no cortical signal:

| | VP002 | VP003 | VP004 | VP005 |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| EEG detector | 0.925 | 0.800 | 0.652 | 0.888 |
| Eyes only | 0.883 | 0.848 | 0.522 | 0.559 |

Two participants where the eyes come close, one where the detector clears them by 0.33. Both
columns published.

**Frozen before scoring.** Protocols written in advance, models hashed before any held-out
block is touched, fitted state verified unchanged afterwards, holdout participant consumed
exactly once.

## The result

**8 of 15 sessions beat the time-shift null at p ≤ 0.05**, 14 of 15 above chance, mean
out-of-sample AUROC 0.65 to 0.93 across five participants.

And the half we find more useful: brief evoked events are undetectable by five methods on
four participants. A zigzag persistent-homology detector matched 6 of 36 events against a
2 of 36 baseline, which reads as a threefold improvement, and sits at p = 0.13 against
randomly thrown darts. Without the null we would have shipped it.

That decided the product. MemoryPalace captures forward on a persistence filter because
sustained states survived the analysis and instantaneous spikes did not.

## Reproduce

```bash
cd eeg-state-detection
python -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install -e '.[test,real]'
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q                                   # 79 tests
.venv/bin/python scripts/fetch_shin.py --subject 2 --out data/shin2018/VP002
.venv/bin/python -m eeg_moments backtest --out outputs/backtest_state_reproduction
```

## What we do not claim

The label is task versus rest, a proxy for a state change, not confusion or insight. Ocular
contribution is not isolated on two participants. Two of five participants are weak. 72
seconds of labeled rest per participant cannot support a false-alarm rate per hour. Weights
are session-specific. These are public participants, unrelated to anyone filmed in the demo.
